  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Auricularia americana, commonly known as Jelly Tree Ear, is a gelatinous fungus characterized by its ear-like or cup-shaped fruiting bodies that are dark brown to blackish in color. This wood-decaying species belongs to the Auriculariaceae family and is found throughout New England where it plays an important ecological role in decomposing dead wood.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Gelatinous texture",
      "description": "The fruiting body has a distinctive jelly-like, rubbery consistency that is moist and flexible, becoming brittle when dry",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Ear or cup shape",
      "description": "Fruiting bodies resemble a human ear or small cup, typically 1-3 inches across, with a somewhat lobed or wrinkled surface",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Dark coloration",
      "description": "The fungus displays a dark brown to blackish color on the outer surface, with a slightly lighter, wrinkled inner surface",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Jelly Tree Ear grows on dead or dying hardwood trees and branches, particularly on oak, beech, and other deciduous wood throughout New England forests and wooded areas.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Unlike most fungi, Auricularia americana is edible and has been used in Asian cuisines for centuries; in New England, it's more commonly appreciated for its role as a decomposer in forest ecosystems.",
